From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 08:21:47 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] qualcommax: ipq60xx: add support for Arista C-200

Hardware specification:
- SoC: Qualcomm IPQ6010 (4x Cortex-A53), on the IPQ6018 AP-CP01-C3
  reference design
- RAM: 1 GiB DDR3L
- Flash: 32 MiB SPI NOR (Winbond W25Q256JW),
  512 MiB NAND (Hynix H27S4G8F2EDA-BC)
- WLAN: IPQ6010: 2.4 GHz 2x2 and 5 GHz 2x2 802.11ax
- Ethernet: lan1 10/100/1000 (Qualcomm Atheros AR8033, SGMII), PoE in
- TPM: Infineon SLB9670 (TPM 2.0), SPI
- LEDs: power, 2.4 GHz, 5 GHz and LAN, each green and red
- Button: reset (pinhole)
- UART: RJ-45 console port, RS-232 levels, 115200 8N1
- Power: 802.3af PoE on lan1, or 12 V DC

Flash layout: the stock firmware keeps two slots, their kernels in SPI
NOR (HL_backup0, HL_backup1) and their roots in NAND ("rootfs",
"rootfs_1"); pri picks the slot. OpenWrt takes NAND slot 0 for UBI
(kernel, rootfs, rootfs_data); slot 1 stays as a fallback. Slot 0 may
be the one the stock firmware runs (pri=0); it is replaced all the
same, and pri=1 makes slot 1 the fallback. The device tree appends
ubi.mtd=rootfs and root=/dev/ubiblock0_1, so the root is found whatever
U-Boot passes.

Flash instructions (console needed):
1. Stop U-Boot: it waits 2 s and stops only for the string
   s6.D5r4Gnb89ZEWSW6Kq.
   Do not let the stock firmware boot meanwhile: its boot script saves
   the U-Boot environment on every boot.
2. Boot the initramfs from a TFTP server:
     setenv ipaddr <AP address>; setenv serverip <TFTP server>
     setenv bootargs console=ttyMSM0,115200n8 rootwait panic=0
     tftpboot 0x60000000 openwrt-qualcommax-ipq60xx-arista_c-200-initramfs-uImage.itb
     bootm 0x60000000#config@cp01-c3
3. Back up all MTD partitions; 0:ART holds the unit's calibration data
   and MAC address and exists nowhere else.
4. Copy the factory image to /tmp and write it to slot 0 ("rootfs",
   mtd0 in /proc/mtd):
     ubidetach -p /dev/mtd0; ubiformat /dev/mtd0 -y -f /tmp/factory.ubi
5. Set the boot command, in one environment write, and reboot:
     fw_setenv -s - <<'EOF'
     owrt_args console=ttyMSM0,115200n8 rootwait panic=10
     owrt_parts setenv mtdids nand0=nand0; setenv mtdparts mtdparts=nand0:0x10000000@0x0(ubi)
     owrt_load ubi part ubi && ubi read 0x44000000 kernel
     owrt_boot run owrt_parts owrt_load && bootm 0x44000000#config@cp01-c3
     arista_unset setenv mtdids; setenv mtdparts
     arista_args if test ${pri} = 0; then setenv bootargs ${ba0}; else setenv bootargs ${ba1}; fi
     arista_boot run arista_unset arista_args ubootscript
     bootcmd setenv bootargs ${owrt_args}; run owrt_boot; run arista_boot
     pri 1
     EOF

The stock boot (bootipq) checks image signatures; this boot command
loads OpenWrt's kernel from UBI instead and falls back to the stock
boot, slot 1. The stock boot script saves the environment, so the
fallback first puts the stock bootargs and partition settings back.

Return to stock: fw_setenv bootcmd 'run ubootscript', then reboot.

MAC addresses:
- lan1: 0:ART 0x18 (label)
- WLAN: from the calibration data

Calibration data for the IPQ6010's radios is read from 0:ART through an
NVMEM cell (0x1000); the board data is ipq-wifi-arista_c-200.

The port is in the LAN by default, with DHCP. The LAN LED is green
while the port has a link, and the power LED green once the system is
up (red while it boots, in failsafe and during an upgrade), as on the
Arista W-318. The TPM is held in reset until gpio79 is driven high, as
the stock firmware does before loading its driver; a gpio-hog does it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
